// Creative Problem Solving lessons 6–10: decision-making strategies, constraints, alternatives and financial soundness,
// teamwork and team dynamics, the 15-minute preparation and the 10-minute presentation.
const RULES=['HOSA Creative Problem Solving 2026–27 guidelines and rating sheet','https://hosa.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/CPS-26-27-8.31_Final.pdf'];
const OS_OB=['OpenStax: Organizational Behavior','https://openstax.org/details/books/organizational-behavior'];
const OS_MGMT=['OpenStax: Principles of Management','https://openstax.org/details/books/principles-management'];
const ASQ_DM=['ASQ: decision matrix','https://asq.org/quality-resources/decision-matrix'];
const AHRQ_TS=['AHRQ: TeamSTEPPS','https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps-program/index.html'];
const CTB_BUDGET=['Community Tool Box: planning and writing an annual budget','https://ctb.ku.edu/en/table-of-contents/finances/managing-finances/annual-budget/main'];
const q=(p,a,b,c,d,e)=>[p,[a,b,c,d],e];
const match=(title,pairs)=>({t:'w',kind:'match',title,pairs});
const order=(title,steps)=>({t:'w',kind:'order',title,steps});
const sort=(title,buckets,items)=>({t:'w',kind:'sort',title,buckets,items});
const flip=(title,cards)=>({t:'w',kind:'flip',title,cards});
const kase=(title,nodes)=>({t:'w',kind:'case',title,start:'a',nodes});
const chk=(q,options,why)=>({t:'check',q,options,why});
const ex=(title,prompt,steps,answer)=>({t:'example',title,prompt,steps,answer});

export const CP6={title:'Problem-solving strategies and decision making',hook:'Every day, health leaders make decisions with incomplete information, limited time and competing goals: which program to fund, how to schedule staff, how to respond to a crisis. Problem-solving strategies and skills make up 24% of the test, and the decision-making process you use in Round Two determines whether your solution is logical and convincing.',objectives:['Describe Adair’s classic decision-making approach and the rational model.','Explain bounded rationality, satisficing and heuristics.','Use a weighted decision matrix to compare options.','Compare individual and group decision making.'],sections:[
['Adair’s classic approach',[
'John Adair’s classic approach to decision making has five steps: **(1) define the objective**—what you want to achieve; **(2) collect relevant information**—facts, data, options already known; **(3) generate feasible options**—realistic alternatives; **(4) make the decision**—evaluate options and choose; and **(5) implement and evaluate**—carry out the decision and check results.',
'Adair emphasizes that good decision makers combine **analytical** thinking (logic, data), **creative** thinking (new options) and **valuing** (judgment about what matters). He also stresses involving others appropriately—sharing decisions with those who must carry them out increases commitment.',
'This sequence fits Round Two well: define the objective from the secret problem, use provided information, generate options, decide and present an implementation and evaluation plan.',
"The same steps apply outside competition: student councils choosing events, hospitals selecting new equipment and public health departments deciding where to place clinics.",
order('Adair’s classic approach',['Define the objective','Collect relevant information','Generate feasible options','Make the decision','Implement and evaluate'])]],
['The rational model and its limits',[
'The **rational decision-making model** assumes decision makers identify the problem, establish **decision criteria**, **weight** them, generate **all** alternatives, evaluate each against the criteria and choose the option with the highest value. It is thorough and logical.',
'Real decisions rarely meet these assumptions. Herbert Simon’s idea of **bounded rationality** recognizes limits on time, information and mental capacity. Decision makers often **satisfice**—choose the first option that is good enough—rather than optimize. McShane and Von Glinow note that people also evaluate alternatives against an **implicit favorite** and are influenced by emotions and biases.',
'In a 15-minute prep, satisficing is unavoidable; the goal is to satisfice **wisely** by setting clear criteria and checking a few strong options.',
match('Concept and meaning',[['Rational model','Systematic choice of the best option using weighted criteria'],['Bounded rationality','Limits on time, information and thinking'],['Satisficing','Choosing an option that is good enough'],['Decision criteria','Standards used to judge options'],['Implicit favorite','A preferred option chosen before full evaluation'],['Optimizing','Seeking the single best possible option']])]],
['Heuristics in decision making',[
'**Heuristics** are mental shortcuts that speed decisions but can mislead. The **availability heuristic** judges likelihood by how easily examples come to mind. The **representativeness heuristic** judges by similarity to a stereotype. **Anchoring and adjustment** starts from an initial value and adjusts insufficiently.',
'Heuristics are useful under time pressure—experienced clinicians use pattern recognition—but they should be checked against data. Recognizing them is also tested directly.',
'A practical safeguard: before finalizing, ask one teammate to play **devil’s advocate** and challenge the choice.',
"Experienced professionals use heuristics wisely by pairing them with checklists and data. Pilots, surgeons and nurses rely on checklists precisely because quick judgments can miss important steps under pressure.",
sort('Heuristic or bias at work?',['Availability','Representativeness','Anchoring'],[['Fearing a rare disease after seeing news coverage',0],['Assuming a quiet student cannot lead because leaders are “outgoing”',1],['Basing a budget on the first number suggested',2],['Overestimating plane crashes after a recent crash',0],['Judging a clinic’s quality by its modern lobby',1]])]],
['The weighted decision matrix',[
'A **weighted decision matrix** compares options objectively. List options in rows and criteria in columns (for example, **cost**, **impact**, **feasibility**, **speed**, **innovation**). Assign each criterion a **weight** reflecting importance, score each option on each criterion (for example, 1–5), multiply score by weight and add the totals. The highest total suggests the best option.',
'Matrices make reasoning visible and are easy to sketch on a flip chart, showing judges that your choice was logical and considered alternatives—supporting the explanation and financial-soundness scores.',
'Keep criteria few and relevant to the problem; use the provided information to set weights.',
ex('Weighted decision matrix','Compare two invented options to reduce missed appointments, with weights cost 3, impact 4, feasibility 2 (scores 1–5).',['Text reminders: cost 5×3=15, impact 4×4=16, feasibility 5×2=10, total 41','Mobile clinic: cost 2×3=6, impact 5×4=20, feasibility 2×2=4, total 30','Text reminders score higher overall','Consider combining with a smaller outreach effort'],'Text reminders (41) outscore a mobile clinic (30) on these weighted criteria (invented for practice).')]],
['Group decision making',[
'Groups bring **more information**, **diverse perspectives** and **greater commitment** to decisions, but they can be slower and suffer from **groupthink**, **social loafing** and pressure to conform. Techniques that improve group decisions include **brainwriting**, the **nominal group technique** (silent idea generation, sharing, discussion, then private voting), **devil’s advocacy** and **constructive conflict** focused on ideas, not people.',
'Groups may decide by **consensus** (everyone can support the decision), **majority vote** or **leader decision after consultation**. In a 15-minute prep, agree in advance how you will decide—often consultation followed by a quick vote—so time is not lost.',
'Decisions that the whole team supports lead to more confident, unified presentations.',
"Agree on your decision rule before prep starts. A common choice is consensus when possible, with a quick majority vote after one minute of discussion if needed.",
sort('Helps or hurts group decisions?',['Helps','Hurts'],[['Nominal group technique',0],['Groupthink',1],['Devil’s advocate',0],['Social loafing',1],['Constructive conflict about ideas',0],['Pressure to agree with the leader',1]])]],
['Implementing and evaluating decisions',[
'A decision is only as good as its implementation. Plan **who** will do **what**, **when**, with what **resources**, and how you will **measure success**. A simple implementation timeline (month 1: pilot; month 2: expand; month 3: evaluate) shows judges your solution is realistic.',
'**Evaluation** checks whether the decision achieved its objective, using measurable indicators such as the percentage of missed appointments, volunteer hours or membership renewals. Plan to adjust if results fall short.',
'Including implementation and evaluation in your presentation demonstrates the complete problem-solving process and strengthens your explanation score.',
order('Implementation plan elements',['Actions and responsible people','Timeline','Resources and budget','Success measures','Evaluation and adjustment'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Choosing between options',{a:{text:'Your team has three ideas and four minutes left. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the fastest logical way to choose?',choices:[['A quick weighted matrix on cost, impact and feasibility','b','Correct.',true],['Choose whichever the loudest member prefers','b','That invites bias.',false]]},b:{text:'The matrix favors text reminders, but one member worries about patients without phones. What do you do?',choices:[['Add a low-cost alternative, such as postcard reminders, for those patients','c','Correct. Address concerns constructively.',true],['Ignore the concern','c','Devil’s advocacy improves decisions.',false]]},c:{text:'Your final solution is logical, inclusive and easy to explain.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Adair’s classic approach: define the objective, collect relevant information, generate feasible options, make the decision, implement and evaluate; combine analytical, creative and valuing thinking.','The rational model uses weighted criteria, but bounded rationality leads people to satisfice; heuristics (availability, representativeness, anchoring) speed decisions but can mislead—use a devil’s advocate.','Use a weighted decision matrix to compare options; improve group decisions with brainwriting, nominal group technique and constructive conflict; present an implementation and evaluation plan.'],terms:[['Satisficing','Choosing an option that is good enough rather than optimal.'],['Bounded rationality','Limits on time, information and thinking in decision making.'],['Weighted decision matrix','A tool that scores options on weighted criteria.']],mcqs:[q('In the classic approach to decision-making, what is the first step?','Define the objective','Generate feasible options','Collect relevant information','Implement and evaluate','Adair.'),q('Choosing an option that is good enough is:','Satisficing','Optimizing','Anchoring','Brainstorming','Bounded rationality.'),q('Who described bounded rationality?','Herbert Simon','John Adair','Edward de Bono','Daniel Kahneman','Limits on rationality.'),q('Judging likelihood by how easily examples come to mind is the:','Availability heuristic','Representativeness heuristic','Anchoring heuristic','Sunk cost fallacy','Recent news.'),q('In a weighted matrix, each score is:','Multiplied by the criterion’s weight','Divided by the number of options','Ignored','Added to the budget','Weight × score.'),q('The nominal group technique ends with:','Private voting','Public criticism','A coin toss','The leader deciding alone','Silent ideas, discussion, vote.'),q('Which hurts group decisions?','Groupthink','Devil’s advocacy','Brainwriting','Constructive conflict','Conformity pressure.'),q('An implementation plan should include:','Actions, timeline, resources and success measures','Only a slogan','Only the problem','Only team names','Complete plan.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would use Adair’s decision-making steps and a weighted decision matrix to choose a solution in Round Two.',rubric:[['Adair steps',['define','objective','collect','information','generate','options','decide','implement','evaluate']],['Matrix',['criteria','weight','score','multiply','total','compare']],['Group process',['brainwriting','vote','devil’s advocate','groupthink','consensus']],['Implementation',['who','when','resources','budget','measure','timeline']]],model:'We would follow Adair’s classic approach. First, we define the objective from the secret problem—for example, reducing missed appointments at a rural clinic from 30% to 15% within six months. Second, we collect relevant information from the materials HOSA provides, such as patient survey results, budget limits and transportation data. Third, we generate feasible options using brainwriting so every member contributes: text reminders, transportation vouchers, evening hours and telehealth visits. Fourth, we make the decision using a quick weighted decision matrix sketched on scrap paper or an index card. We choose criteria that match the problem and rubric—cost (weight 3), impact on missed appointments (weight 4), feasibility (weight 2) and innovation (weight 1)—score each option from 1 to 5, multiply each score by its weight and total them. If text reminders and telehealth score highest, we may combine them. Before finalizing, one teammate plays devil’s advocate to check for bias or missing groups, such as patients without smartphones, and we adjust the plan. Fifth, we present how the solution would be implemented and evaluated: who is responsible, a three-month timeline from pilot to full launch, a budget that fits the provided limit and measures such as the monthly missed-appointment rate, with a plan to adjust if results fall short.'},sources:[RULES,OS_OB,ASQ_DM]};

export const CP7={title:'Examining constraints, alternatives and financial soundness',hook:'An idea that would cost $2 million cannot solve a problem with a $10,000 budget. A plan that needs a year cannot fix a crisis next month. Examining constraints and developing alternatives makes up 20% of the test, and judges award 10 points for a financially sound solution. Realism is what turns a creative idea into a winning one.',objectives:['Identify common constraints on health-related solutions.','Develop and compare alternatives within constraints.','Build a simple, realistic budget and cost justification.','Assess risks and plan contingencies.'],sections:[
['Types of constraints',[
'**Constraints** are limits a solution must respect. Common types include **financial** (budget, funding sources), **time** (deadlines, how quickly results are needed), **people** (staff, volunteers, skills), **physical** (space, equipment, location), **legal and regulatory** (privacy laws, licensing, school policies), **ethical** (fairness, consent) and **organizational** (leadership approval, culture).',
'The secret problem often states constraints directly (“a budget of $5,000,” “within one semester”) or implies them (“a small rural clinic with two nurses”). Read carefully and list every constraint before choosing a solution.',
'Some constraints are **hard** (cannot be changed, such as laws) and others are **soft** (could be negotiated, such as schedules). Creative solutions sometimes challenge soft constraints—but never ignore hard ones.',
"Write constraints on an index card during prep and check every idea against the card. This simple habit prevents the common mistake of presenting a solution that breaks a stated limit.",
sort('Type of constraint',['Financial','Time','People','Legal or regulatory'],[['$5,000 budget',0],['Must start within one month',1],['Only two nurses on staff',2],['Patient privacy rules',3],['Grant ends in June',1],['No volunteers trained in CPR',2]])]],
['Developing alternatives',[
'Never settle on one idea without considering alternatives. Developing several options shows judges you examined the problem thoroughly and helps you find a solution that fits constraints. Alternatives can differ in **scale** (small pilot vs full program), **approach** (education vs environmental change), **cost** and **timeline**.',
'A useful method is to create a **low-cost**, a **moderate** and an **ambitious** version of your best idea, then choose the one that best balances impact and constraints—or phase them, starting small and expanding if successful.',
'Mention briefly in your presentation the alternatives you considered and why you chose your solution; it strengthens understanding and explanation scores.',
order('Developing alternatives',['List the constraints','Generate several options','Create low-cost, moderate and ambitious versions','Compare against criteria','Choose or phase the best option','Explain why alternatives were not chosen'])]],
['Building a realistic budget',[
'A financially sound solution shows **what it costs**, **how it will be funded** and **why it is worth it**. List major costs—**personnel** (staff time, stipends), **materials** (printing, supplies), **technology** (software, devices), **space**, **marketing** and **evaluation**—and estimate amounts reasonably. Use numbers provided in the problem whenever possible.',
'Identify **funding sources**: existing budgets, grants, partnerships, donations, sponsorships or in-kind contributions (donated space or volunteer time). Show that the total fits the budget, with a small **contingency** reserve for unexpected costs.',
'Explain value with a simple **cost-benefit** or **cost per person** comparison: “For $4,200, we can reach 600 patients—about $7 per patient.”',
"Round numbers to make budgets easy to follow, and show totals clearly. Judges should see at a glance that the solution fits within the limit.",
ex('A simple solution budget','Build an invented budget for a text-reminder program with a $5,000 limit.',['Software subscription: $1,800','Staff training (4 hours × 3 staff): $360','Postcard reminders for patients without phones: $600','Promotional flyers: $240','Evaluation survey printing: $100','Contingency (about 10%): $400','Total: $3,500, under the $5,000 limit'],'A realistic budget with a contingency that fits the constraint (figures invented for practice).')]],
['Cost-benefit and return on investment',[
'**Cost-benefit analysis** compares a solution’s costs with its expected benefits, ideally in similar units. In health, benefits might include fewer missed appointments, reduced emergency visits, improved outcomes or increased revenue. **Return on investment (ROI)** = (benefit − cost) ÷ cost.',
'Even rough estimates are persuasive when reasonable and explained: “Each missed appointment costs the clinic about $150 in lost revenue (from the provided data). Reducing missed appointments by 100 per year saves about $15,000, compared with a $3,500 program cost.”',
'Avoid inventing precise figures not supported by the problem; use clearly labeled estimates and the data provided.',
chk('If a program costs $3,500 and produces $15,000 in savings, what is the net benefit?',['$11,500','$18,500','$3,500','$15,000'],'$15,000 − $3,500 = $11,500.')]],
['Risk assessment and contingency planning',[
'Every solution has **risks**: low participation, technology failure, cost overruns, staff turnover, privacy concerns. Identify the most important risks, estimate their **likelihood** and **impact** and plan **mitigation** (reduce the risk) and **contingencies** (what to do if it happens).',
'A **SWOT analysis**—**strengths**, **weaknesses** (internal), **opportunities** and **threats** (external)—helps examine a solution’s position quickly. Mentioning one or two risks and how you would handle them shows judges mature, realistic thinking.',
'Risk planning also supports financial soundness by showing how you would protect resources.',
"Mention one risk and its mitigation in your presentation, such as “If enrollment is low in the first month, we will add school-based sign-up days.” A brief contingency shows judges you have thought beyond the ideal scenario.",
sort('SWOT category',['Strength','Weakness','Opportunity','Threat'],[['Low cost using existing software',0],['Some patients lack smartphones',1],['A state grant for telehealth is available',2],['Possible privacy breach',3],['Staff already trained in texting tools',0],['Funding cuts next year',3]])]],
['Stakeholders and ethics',[
'Consider **stakeholders**—everyone affected by or influencing the solution: patients, families, staff, administrators, funders, community partners and regulators. Ask what each needs and how they might react. Solutions that ignore key stakeholders often fail.',
'Check **ethical** and **legal** constraints: patient **privacy** (for example, HIPAA in health settings), **consent**, **equity** (does the solution exclude anyone?) and **safety**. Judges value solutions that are fair and responsible as well as creative.',
'A brief stakeholder and ethics check during prep can prevent a fatal flaw in an otherwise strong solution.',
match('Stakeholder and likely concern',[['Patients','Convenience and privacy'],['Staff','Workload and training'],['Administrators','Cost and outcomes'],['Funders','Measurable results'],['Community partners','Shared goals and recognition'],['Regulators','Legal compliance']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Budget check',{a:{text:'Your team’s solution includes buying 50 tablets at $400 each, but the problem’s budget is $10,000. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the issue?',choices:[['Tablets alone cost $20,000—double the budget','b','Correct.',true],['No issue','b','50 × $400 = $20,000.',false]]},b:{text:'What is the best fix?',choices:[['Use a smaller pilot, shared devices or partner donations to fit the budget with a contingency','c','Correct.',true],['Ignore the budget','c','Financial soundness is scored.',false]]},c:{text:'Your revised plan fits the budget and explains how to expand later with grant funding.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Constraints include financial, time, people, physical, legal and regulatory, ethical and organizational limits; list them all, respect hard constraints and challenge soft ones creatively.','Develop low-cost, moderate and ambitious alternatives, compare them against criteria and explain why you chose one; build a realistic budget (personnel, materials, technology, marketing, evaluation, contingency) with funding sources.','Show value with cost-benefit, ROI or cost per person using provided data; assess risks with SWOT and contingency plans; consider stakeholders, privacy, equity and safety.'],terms:[['Constraint','A limit that a solution must respect.'],['Return on investment','(Benefit − cost) ÷ cost.'],['Contingency','A reserve or plan for unexpected problems.']],mcqs:[q('A $5,000 budget is a:','Financial constraint','Time constraint','People constraint','Legal constraint','Money limit.'),q('Patient privacy rules are a:','Legal or regulatory constraint','Financial constraint','Time constraint','Creative constraint','HIPAA.'),q('ROI is calculated as:','(Benefit − cost) ÷ cost','Cost ÷ benefit','Benefit × cost','Cost − benefit','Return on investment.'),q('If 50 tablets cost $400 each, the total is:','$20,000','$4,500','$2,000','$40,000','50 × 400.'),q('In SWOT, “threats” are:','External risks','Internal strengths','Internal weaknesses','External opportunities','Outside factors.'),q('Why include a contingency in a budget?','To cover unexpected costs','To spend more','It is not needed','To impress judges with large numbers','Risk planning.'),q('A soft constraint is one that:','Could be negotiated','Can never change','Is always legal','Has no effect','Example: schedules.'),q('Cost per person is useful for:','Showing value','Hiding costs','Choosing team roles','Writing a charter','Efficiency.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would make sure a Round Two solution is realistic and financially sound, including constraints, alternatives, budget, cost-benefit and risks.',rubric:[['Constraints',['financial','time','people','legal','hard','soft','list']],['Alternatives',['alternatives','low-cost','pilot','compare','criteria','phase']],['Budget and value',['budget','costs','funding','contingency','cost-benefit','cost per','roi']],['Risk and stakeholders',['risk','swot','mitigation','stakeholders','privacy','equity']]],model:'As soon as we read the secret problem, we would list every constraint: the budget, the timeline, available staff and volunteers, space and equipment, legal rules such as patient privacy and organizational approvals. We would separate hard constraints we must respect from soft constraints we might creatively work around. Next, we would develop alternatives—a low-cost, a moderate and an ambitious version of our best idea—and compare them against criteria such as cost, impact and feasibility, choosing the one that fits or phasing them by starting with a pilot. To show financial soundness, we would build a simple budget on our flip chart with personnel, materials, technology, marketing, evaluation and a contingency of about 10%, using numbers from the problem where possible, and identify funding sources such as the existing budget, a grant, partner donations or in-kind volunteer time. We would demonstrate value with a cost-benefit comparison or cost per person—for example, a $3,500 reminder program that saves about $15,000 in lost revenue from missed appointments—clearly labeling estimates. We would assess risks using a quick SWOT, naming the biggest risks, such as low participation or technology failure, and explaining how we would reduce or respond to them. Finally, we would check stakeholders and ethics, making sure the solution protects privacy, includes patients without smartphones and has staff support, so judges see a creative solution that is also realistic and responsible.'},sources:[RULES,OS_MGMT,CTB_BUDGET]};

export const CP8={title:'Teamwork and team dynamics',hook:'Two teams with equally smart members can perform very differently. One divides tasks, listens and builds on ideas; the other argues, waits for the leader and runs out of time. Organizational Behavior—one of your official references—explains why. Understanding team dynamics improves your test score and your 15-minute prep.',objectives:['Explain team charters, roles and norms.','Describe Tuckman’s stages of team development.','Recognize threats to team performance such as groupthink and social loafing.','Use communication and conflict skills to build effective teams.'],sections:[
['What makes an effective team',[
'A **team** is two or more people who interact, influence each other, share a common purpose and are mutually accountable for results. Effective teams have a **clear goal**, **complementary skills**, **defined roles**, **trust**, **open communication** and **commitment**.',
'Research summarized in McShane and Von Glinow’s **Organizational Behavior** emphasizes that team effectiveness depends on **team design** (size, diversity, task structure), **team processes** (development, norms, cohesion, trust) and the **organizational environment**. Teams of moderate size with diverse but compatible members often perform best.',
'**Psychological safety**—the belief that members can speak up and take risks without embarrassment—is strongly linked to team learning and creativity.',
"Diversity of background and thinking style helps teams generate more ideas, but only when members trust each other enough to share them.",
sort('Supports team effectiveness?',['Supports','Undermines'],[['Clear shared goal',0],['Members afraid to share ideas',1],['Defined roles',0],['One member doing all the work',1],['Trust and open communication',0],['No accountability',1]])]],
['Team charters',[
'A **team charter** is a document that sets out how a team will work together. It typically includes the team’s **purpose and goals**, members’ **roles and strengths**, agreed **communication methods**, **decision-making** and **conflict resolution** processes, **norms** (expected behaviors) and how members’ **contributions will be measured**.',
'McShane and Von Glinow describe the charter as a product of assessing team members’ strengths, agreeing on communication methods and measuring contributions. Writing one at the start of the competition season prevents misunderstandings and helps the team move quickly through early stages of development.',
'Your Creative Problem Solving team should create a charter that includes roles for Round Two prep.',
order('Creating a team charter',['State the team’s purpose and goals','Assess members’ strengths and assign roles','Agree on communication methods','Decide how decisions will be made','Set norms and conflict resolution steps','Agree on how contributions will be measured'])]],
['Stages of team development',[
'Bruce Tuckman’s model describes stages teams often pass through: **Forming** (members get acquainted, polite and uncertain), **Storming** (conflict over roles, ideas and leadership), **Norming** (agreement on roles and norms, growing cohesion), **Performing** (focused, effective work) and **Adjourning** (wrapping up when the task ends).',
'Teams do not always move in a straight line; new members or stress can send a team back to storming. Practicing together throughout the year helps your team reach the performing stage before competition.',
'Recognizing the stage your team is in helps you respond: clarify goals when forming, address conflict constructively when storming and protect good habits when performing.',
"Teams can speed through storming by agreeing on norms early and addressing disagreements openly. A charter written in the forming stage provides the shared rules that make norming easier.",
order('Tuckman’s stages',['Forming','Storming','Norming','Performing','Adjourning'])]],
['Roles in a team',[
'Teams need both **task roles** (getting work done) and **maintenance roles** (keeping relationships healthy). Task roles include **initiator** (proposes ideas), **information seeker**, **coordinator**, **evaluator** and **recorder**. Maintenance roles include **encourager**, **harmonizer** (eases tension), **gatekeeper** (ensures everyone speaks) and **compromiser**.',
'For Round Two, assign specific prep roles: **timekeeper**, **facilitator** (keeps the process moving), **recorder** (writes on index cards), **designer** (prepares flip charts) and **analyst** (reviews provided information and numbers). Rotate roles in practice so everyone can fill any role.',
'Avoid **dysfunctional roles** such as dominating, blocking or withdrawing.',
"Rotating roles also builds empathy: the member who usually designs charts learns how hard timekeeping is, and the timekeeper learns how much time good charts require.",
match('Team role and contribution',[['Facilitator','Keeps the process moving'],['Timekeeper','Tracks prep time'],['Recorder','Writes ideas and notes'],['Designer','Prepares flip charts'],['Analyst','Reviews provided data and budget'],['Gatekeeper','Ensures every member contributes']])]],
['Threats to team performance',[
'**Groupthink** occurs when a cohesive group values agreement over critical evaluation, leading to poor decisions. Signs include pressure to conform, self-censorship and illusions of unanimity. **Social loafing** happens when members exert less effort in a group than alone because individual contributions are less visible.',
'Other threats include **process losses** (time spent coordinating), **dominant members** who silence others and **unresolved conflict**. Countermeasures: assign a **devil’s advocate**, use **brainwriting**, make individual contributions **visible**, set clear roles and hold members accountable.',
'Because the rubric scores **team participation**, a team where one person dominates loses points even with a good solution.',
"Teams can also build cohesion outside practice—sharing a meal, celebrating progress or volunteering together. Cohesive teams communicate more openly and recover faster from mistakes, which matters during a stressful 15-minute prep. Cohesion should never become pressure to agree, however; healthy teams combine closeness with honest debate.",
sort('Threat or countermeasure?',['Threat','Countermeasure'],[['Groupthink',0],['Assigning a devil’s advocate',1],['Social loafing',0],['Making individual tasks visible',1],['A member dominating discussion',0],['Brainwriting before discussion',1]])]],
['Communication and conflict',[
'Effective team communication is **clear**, **concise** and **respectful**. In health care, tools like AHRQ’s **TeamSTEPPS** promote **closed-loop communication** (repeat back instructions), **SBAR** (situation, background, assessment, recommendation) and **check-backs** to prevent errors. The same habits help a prep team stay coordinated.',
'**Constructive conflict** (task conflict about ideas) can improve decisions; **relationship conflict** (personal) usually harms teams. Keep disagreements focused on ideas, listen to understand, and look for **integrative** (win–win) solutions. Common conflict-handling styles include problem solving, forcing, avoiding, yielding and compromising.',
'Agree in your charter on how to resolve disagreements quickly during prep, such as a brief vote after one minute of discussion.',
"Feedback is another key team skill. Use specific, kind and useful feedback after practices: describe what happened, its effect and a suggestion, such as “When we skipped the budget, the judges asked about cost; let’s assign it earlier.”",
match('Conflict-handling style and description',[['Problem solving','Seeks a win–win solution'],['Forcing','Pushes one’s own view'],['Avoiding','Sidesteps the conflict'],['Yielding','Gives in to others'],['Compromising','Each side gives up something'],['Task conflict','Disagreement about ideas']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Team trouble',{a:{text:'In practice, one member makes every decision while others stay quiet. (Scenario invented for practice.) What does the team risk?',choices:[['Groupthink and a lower team participation score','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing—it saves time','b','Participation and diverse ideas matter.',false]]},b:{text:'What change helps most?',choices:[['Assign prep roles, use brainwriting and appoint a gatekeeper','c','Correct.',true],['Let the dominant member present alone','c','All members must take an active role.',false]]},c:{text:'Your next practice includes every member and produces stronger ideas.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Effective teams have clear goals, complementary skills, defined roles, trust and psychological safety; team design, processes and environment shape performance.','A team charter—produced by assessing members’ strengths, agreeing on communication methods and measuring contributions—sets purpose, roles, norms, decisions and conflict steps; Tuckman’s stages are forming, storming, norming, performing and adjourning.','Assign task and maintenance roles (facilitator, timekeeper, recorder, designer, analyst, gatekeeper); counter groupthink and social loafing; use closed-loop communication and constructive, idea-focused conflict.'],terms:[['Team charter','A document defining a team’s purpose, roles, communication and processes.'],['Groupthink','Valuing agreement over critical evaluation.'],['Social loafing','Putting in less effort in a group than alone.']],mcqs:[q('What document is produced by assessing the team member’s strengths, agreeing on communication methods, and measuring their contributions?','Team Charter','Team Vision','Team Identity','Team Mission Statement','HOSA sample question (McShane and Von Glinow).'),q('Which is the second stage in Tuckman’s model?','Storming','Forming','Norming','Performing','Conflict stage.'),q('Putting in less effort in a group is:','Social loafing','Groupthink','Norming','Brainstorming','Less visible effort.'),q('Which role ensures every member speaks?','Gatekeeper','Recorder','Timekeeper','Designer','Maintenance role.'),q('Psychological safety means:','Members can speak up without fear of embarrassment','No one disagrees','The leader decides alone','Only experts talk','Supports creativity.'),q('Closed-loop communication involves:','Repeating back instructions','Ignoring messages','Speaking all at once','Only written notes','TeamSTEPPS.'),q('Which conflict style seeks a win–win solution?','Problem solving','Forcing','Avoiding','Yielding','Integrative.'),q('Which countermeasure reduces groupthink?','Assigning a devil’s advocate','Pressuring members to agree','Removing discussion','Hiding individual work','Critical evaluation.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your Creative Problem Solving team would use a team charter, roles and conflict skills to perform well in the 15-minute prep and presentation.',rubric:[['Charter',['charter','purpose','strengths','roles','communication','decision','contributions']],['Roles',['facilitator','timekeeper','recorder','designer','analyst','gatekeeper','rotate']],['Threats',['groupthink','social loafing','dominate','devil’s advocate','brainwriting']],['Communication and conflict',['closed-loop','respect','task conflict','problem solving','vote','participation']]],model:'At the start of the season, our team would write a team charter. We would state our purpose—to place at ILC by combining strong test scores with a creative, realistic solution—assess each member’s strengths and assign roles, agree to communicate through a group chat and weekly practices, decide that in prep we will discuss for one minute and then vote if we disagree, set norms such as respecting every idea and meeting deadlines and agree to track each member’s practice test scores and contributions. For Round Two prep, we would assign roles we have rotated in practice: a facilitator to keep the process moving, a timekeeper who watches the clock and announces checkpoints, an analyst who studies the provided data and budget, a recorder who writes ideas and speaker notes on index cards and a designer who creates the flip charts, with the facilitator also acting as gatekeeper so everyone contributes. To avoid groupthink, one member plays devil’s advocate before we finalize, and we start idea generation with brainwriting so quieter members are heard; making each person’s task visible prevents social loafing. We would use closed-loop communication, repeating back assignments such as “I’ll draw the budget chart,” and keep any conflict focused on ideas, seeking win–win solutions. Because the rubric rewards equal participation, every member would present one section, showing a united team.'},sources:[RULES,OS_OB,AHRQ_TS]};

export const CP9={title:'The 15-minute preparation',hook:'Fifteen minutes is not much time to understand a new problem, generate ideas, choose a solution, plan a budget, design two flip charts and assign speaking parts. Teams that improvise run out of time; teams with a rehearsed routine finish with minutes to spare. This lesson gives you a minute-by-minute prep plan and flip-chart strategies.',objectives:['Follow a minute-by-minute plan for the 15-minute preparation.','Use provided information effectively.','Design clear, persuasive flip charts.','Prepare index-card notes and speaking roles.'],sections:[
['What you have in the prep room',[
'In the preparation room you may use **resources provided by HOSA** (data, research or background on the secret topic), **two blank flip-chart sheets** (provided by HOSA), **markers** (bring your own—several colors) and **index cards** for notes (bring your own). The section leader announces when **5 minutes** and **1 minute** remain.',
'After 15 minutes, teams are escorted to another room for the presentation and may bring **only** the index cards and flip charts prepared during prep. No other materials are allowed.',
'Pack a kit: a set of bold markers (black, blue, red, green), blank index cards, pencils and a rubber band to keep cards in order.',
flip('Prep room essentials',[['Provided by HOSA','Secret topic resources and two flip-chart sheets'],['Bring','Markers and blank index cards'],['Time warnings','5 minutes and 1 minute'],['Into the presentation','Only prepared index cards and flip charts'],['Not allowed','Any other materials'],['Ethics','Do not reveal the secret topic']])]],
['A minute-by-minute plan',[
'**0:00–2:00 Understand:** read the problem and resources; the analyst highlights key data and constraints. **2:00–4:00 Define:** agree on a problem statement and root causes. **4:00–7:00 Generate and choose:** brainwrite ideas, then quickly evaluate and choose (and possibly combine) a solution. **7:00–10:00 Plan:** outline implementation steps, budget and success measures. **10:00–13:00 Build:** the designer draws flip charts while others write index cards and assign sections. **13:00–15:00 Rehearse:** quick run-through of the opening, transitions and close.',
'Adapt the plan if the problem is simple or complex, but protect time for **planning** and **rehearsal**—these produce the explanation and delivery points.',
'Practice this routine many times so it becomes automatic.',
"Write the routine on a card in your kit as a reminder, and have the timekeeper read each checkpoint aloud. Under stress, a visible plan keeps everyone aligned.",
order('15-minute prep routine',['Understand the problem and data (2 min)','Define the problem and root causes (2 min)','Generate and choose a solution (3 min)','Plan implementation, budget and measures (3 min)','Build flip charts and index cards (3 min)','Rehearse opening, transitions and close (2 min)'])]],
['Using the provided information',[
'Judges award **10 points** for clear and effective use of the information provided in the secret topic. Read resources carefully, mark key **numbers**, **constraints** and **stakeholder** details and use them throughout your solution: in the problem statement, causes, budget and expected results.',
'Cite the provided data explicitly in your presentation: “According to the survey provided, 62% of patients said transportation was their main barrier.” Using the data shows understanding and makes your solution more convincing.',
'If information is missing, state reasonable **assumptions** clearly rather than inventing precise facts.',
"Highlight or underline the most important numbers in the provided materials during the first two minutes so they are easy to find again when building charts and cards. Note the source of each number so you can cite it accurately during the presentation.",
chk('Which statement best uses the provided information?',['“The provided survey shows 62% of patients cite transportation, so our solution targets transportation.”','“We think transportation might matter.”','“Our idea is cool.”','“We ignored the data to be creative.”'],'Cite and use the data.')]],
['Designing flip charts',[
'You have **two flip-chart sheets**. A strong layout: **Sheet 1**—a bold **title** or solution name, the **problem statement** with key data and **root causes**. **Sheet 2**—the **solution** with three to five key components, a **simple budget** and a **timeline** or **success measures**. Visual elements such as icons, arrows, simple charts or a decision matrix help judges follow your logic.',
'Write **large** and **legibly** in dark colors, use **headings** and **bullet points** rather than paragraphs and leave white space. Color-code sections consistently. Judges should be able to read the charts from several feet away.',
'Charts support your speech; they should not contain everything you say.',
"Sketch the layout in pencil first if time allows, then trace in marker. This prevents crowded charts and misspelled headings that are hard to fix.",
sort('Good flip-chart practice?',['Good','Poor'],[['Large, dark headings',0],['Paragraphs of small text',1],['A simple budget table',0],['Light yellow marker for key words',1],['Icons and arrows showing flow',0],['Crowding every inch of the sheet',1]])]],
['Index cards and speaking roles',[
'Every member must take an **active role** in the presentation. During prep, assign sections—for example: **Speaker 1** opening and problem; **Speaker 2** causes and data; **Speaker 3** solution and innovation; **Speaker 4** implementation and budget; **Speaker 5** measures, prior knowledge and close. With fewer members, combine sections.',
'Each speaker writes **key words** and **numbers** on index cards, not scripts, and notes the **transition** to the next speaker. Number the cards and keep them in order.',
'Decide who will point to flip charts and where each member will stand to avoid confusion in the presentation room.',
"Agree on a simple signal, such as a nod, for when a speaker is finishing and the next should step forward. Smooth handoffs save seconds and look professional.",
match('Speaker and section',[['Speaker 1','Opening and problem statement'],['Speaker 2','Root causes and provided data'],['Speaker 3','The innovative solution'],['Speaker 4','Implementation and budget'],['Speaker 5','Measures, prior knowledge and close'],['All','Transitions and team participation']])]],
['Managing time and stress',[
'Assign a **timekeeper** who calls checkpoints (“Four minutes—we need to choose”). Use the **5-minute** warning as your signal to finish charts and the **1-minute** warning to stop and gather materials. Do not keep perfecting charts at the expense of rehearsal.',
'Stay calm and focused: if the team disagrees, use your charter’s quick decision rule. If the problem seems unfamiliar, fall back on your routine—define, find causes, generate, choose, plan.',
'Practice under realistic conditions with a timer and a surprise problem to build confidence.',
"After each practice, record how long each prep stage actually took. Over time, your team will learn where it tends to run long and can adjust the routine to protect planning and rehearsal.",
order('Using the time warnings',['Before 5 minutes: finish planning and most of the charts','At 5 minutes: complete charts and cards','Rehearse opening and transitions','At 1 minute: stop and gather materials','Walk to the presentation room ready'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Running behind',{a:{text:'At the 5-minute warning, your team is still debating between two solutions. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Use your quick decision rule, choose or combine, and move to charts and cards','b','Correct.',true],['Keep debating','b','You need time to prepare the presentation.',false]]},b:{text:'Your second flip chart is half finished at 1 minute. What next?',choices:[['Stop, gather charts and cards and explain the missing details verbally','c','Correct.',true],['Keep drawing after time','c','Follow the timing rules.',false]]},c:{text:'You present confidently, covering the missing details in your speech.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Prep room: HOSA resources and two flip charts provided; bring markers and index cards; warnings at 5 and 1 minute; only prepared cards and charts go to the presentation; do not reveal the topic.','Routine: understand (2 min), define and find causes (2), generate and choose (3), plan implementation, budget and measures (3), build charts and cards (3), rehearse (2); use and cite the provided data (10 points).','Flip charts: Sheet 1 problem, data and causes; Sheet 2 solution, budget and timeline; large, dark, bulleted and visual; assign speaking sections so every member participates; use the timekeeper and warnings.'],terms:[['Flip chart','A large sheet of paper used to display key points.'],['Timekeeper','The team member who tracks time and calls checkpoints.'],['Assumption','A reasonable statement made when information is missing.']],mcqs:[q('How many flip-chart sheets does HOSA provide?','Two','One','Four','None','Plus your markers.'),q('Who provides markers?','The competitors','HOSA','The judges','The section leader','Bring your own.'),q('Which time warnings are given in prep?','5 minutes and 1 minute','10 and 5 minutes','Only at the end','None','Section leader.'),q('What may be brought into the presentation room?','Prepared index cards and flip charts','Phones','The provided resources','Laptops','Nothing else.'),q('How many points is use of provided information worth?','10','15','5','20','Rubric item.'),q('Which flip-chart practice is best?','Large, dark headings and bullet points','Paragraphs in small text','Light yellow key words','No headings','Readable.'),q('What should index cards contain?','Key words and numbers','A full script','Nothing','The entire problem text','Notes.'),q('What should you do if information is missing?','State reasonable assumptions clearly','Invent precise facts','Ignore the gap','Stop the presentation','Honest assumptions.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe your team’s minute-by-minute plan for the 15-minute preparation and how you would design your two flip charts.',rubric:[['Plan',['understand','define','causes','generate','choose','plan','build','rehearse','minutes']],['Data use',['provided','data','constraints','cite','assumptions']],['Flip charts',['two','problem','causes','solution','budget','timeline','large','headings']],['Roles and time',['timekeeper','index cards','speakers','transitions','5 minutes','1 minute']]],model:'In the first two minutes, everyone reads the secret problem while our analyst highlights key data, constraints and stakeholders in the provided resources. From minutes 2 to 4, we agree on a problem statement and use a quick 5 Whys to identify root causes. From minutes 4 to 7, we brainwrite ideas on index cards, share them without criticism and use a quick impact–effort check to choose or combine a solution. From minutes 7 to 10, we plan implementation steps, a simple budget that fits the stated limit with a contingency and success measures. From minutes 10 to 13, our designer builds the flip charts while the others write speaker cards, and from minutes 13 to 15 we rehearse the opening, transitions and close. Our timekeeper calls checkpoints, and at the 5-minute warning we finish charts and cards; at 1 minute we stop and gather materials. Flip chart 1 has a bold solution name, the problem statement with two or three key statistics from the provided data and the root causes, shown with arrows. Flip chart 2 shows the solution’s three to five components with icons, a small budget table, a timeline from pilot to full launch and success measures. We write in large, dark letters with clear headings and bullet points so judges can read them from several feet away. Each of our five members takes one section of the presentation, with key words and numbers on numbered index cards.'},sources:[RULES,OS_MGMT]};

export const CP10={title:'The 10-minute presentation and delivery',hook:'Ten minutes is long enough to explain a thoughtful solution and short enough that every minute counts. Judges score understanding, innovation, explanation, prior knowledge, financial soundness, use of information and delivery. A clear structure, confident teamwork and a memorable close turn your 15 minutes of prep into a winning presentation.',objectives:['Structure a 10-minute solution presentation that addresses every rubric item.','Connect prior knowledge and experience to the solution.','Deliver with strong voice, presence and team participation.','Prepare for the test and presentation through full practice rounds.'],sections:[
['Presentation structure',[
'A sample 10-minute plan: **Opening (0:00–1:00)**—hook and solution name. **Problem and causes (1:00–2:30)**—problem statement, key data and root causes. **Solution (2:30–5:00)**—the innovative solution, its components and why it addresses the causes. **Implementation (5:00–6:30)**—who, what, when. **Budget and value (6:30–7:45)**—costs, funding, cost-benefit. **Measures and prior knowledge (7:45–9:00)**—how success is measured and what experiences informed the solution. **Close (9:00–9:45)**—summary and memorable final line.',
'The timekeeper shows a card at **1 minute remaining** and stops the presentation at **10 minutes**. Aim to finish around **9:30–9:45**.',
'Every section should map to at least one rubric item so no points are missed.',
"Assign the opening and closing to confident speakers, and memorize both. A strong first and last impression frames the judges’ view of everything in between.",
order('10-minute presentation flow',['Opening and solution name','Problem, data and root causes','The innovative solution','Implementation plan','Budget and value','Measures and prior knowledge','Memorable close'])]],
['Showing understanding and innovation',[
'To earn top **understanding** points (15), show a deep, insightful grasp of the problem: explain **root causes**, **stakeholders** and the **context**, citing the provided information. To earn top **innovation** points (15), explain what is **new** about your solution—an unexpected combination, a borrowed analogy, a reframed approach—and why it is better than the obvious answer.',
'State the innovation explicitly: “Instead of asking patients to come to the clinic, our solution brings the clinic to them through a weekly telehealth booth at the library.” Judges should not have to guess what is creative.',
'Briefly mention alternatives you considered and why yours is stronger.',
"Practice explaining your innovation in a single sentence before the presentation. If the team cannot say what is new in one sentence, judges will struggle to identify it too, and innovation points may be lost.",
sort('Shows understanding or innovation?',['Understanding','Innovation'],[['Explaining that transportation is the root cause',0],['A telehealth booth at the library',1],['Citing the provided survey data',0],['Borrowing a ride-share model for clinic visits',1],['Identifying stakeholders and their needs',0],['Combining reminders with transportation vouchers',1]])]],
['Explaining clearly and logically',[
'**Explanation of the solution** (15) rewards a clear, concise, logical, well-constructed presentation. Use **signposting** (“First…, next…, finally…”), connect each part to the problem (“Because transportation is the main barrier, our first component…”) and point to your flip charts at the right moments.',
'Avoid jargon unless you explain it, and keep sentences short. Each speaker should state a clear main point, support it with one or two details and transition smoothly to the next speaker.',
'A logical chain—problem, causes, solution, implementation, budget, results—makes the explanation easy to follow.',
"Point to the relevant part of the flip chart as you speak, then turn back to the judges. Charts reinforce your words; they should never replace eye contact.",
chk('Which transition best supports a logical explanation?',['“Because transportation is the main barrier, our first component provides ride vouchers.”','“Um, next thing.”','“Anyway…”','“I forgot what I was going to say.”'],'Connect each part to the problem.')]],
['Applying prior knowledge and experience',[
'Judges award **15 points** for applying **previous knowledge and experience** to the current problem. Connect your solution to things you know: health science coursework (for example, how chronic disease management requires regular visits), HOSA experiences (a chapter service project that used text reminders), community examples, research you have read or professional practices (such as appointment reminder systems used by real clinics).',
'Be specific: “In our HOSA chapter, switching meeting reminders from email to text raised attendance from 45% to 80%, which shows reminders can change behavior.” Real examples make solutions credible.',
'Plan in prep which member will share which experience, so each connection is natural and brief.',
match('Prior knowledge source and example',[['Health science class','Chronic disease needs regular follow-up'],['HOSA experience','Text reminders raised meeting attendance'],['Community example','A local library’s tech help desk'],['Research','Reminder systems reduce missed appointments'],['Personal experience','Family member’s transportation barriers'],['Professional practice','Clinics using patient portals']])]],
['Delivery and team participation',[
'Delivery is worth **20 points**: **voice** (volume, varied rate, pauses), **stage presence** (poise, posture, eye contact, purposeful gestures, enthusiasm), **diction, pronunciation and grammar** (clear words, no fillers) and **team participation** (each member speaks and carries an equal part).',
'Stand where judges can see both the speaker and the flip charts. Face the judges, not the charts. Speak with energy—enthusiasm suggests confidence in your solution. Keep transitions smooth and avoid long silences.',
'Because every member must take an active role, practice handoffs until they feel natural.',
"If a teammate loses their place, another member can bridge with a short summary and hand back. Practicing this recovery makes the team look calm and unified even when something goes wrong.",
sort('Strong or weak delivery?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Facing judges while pointing to the chart',0],['Reading index cards word for word',1],['Varied volume and confident pace',0],['Frequent “um” and “like”',1],['Each member speaking about two minutes',0],['One member answering everything',1]])]],
['Preparing for the test and full rounds',[
'The **team test average** is added to the final score, so balance presentation practice with test study. Study by test-plan area, focusing on the largest sections—critical thinking (26%) and problem-solving strategies (24%)—and on terms from the four references. Write practice questions and quiz each other weekly.',
'Run **full mock rounds**: a timed test, then a surprise practice problem with 15 minutes of prep and a 10-minute presentation scored with the official rubric by teachers or other HOSA members. Debrief each round: What did judges notice? Which rubric items were weak? How did timing go?',
'Over the season, aim for consistent routines, strong charts and confident, balanced presentations.',
"Keep a log of every practice problem with scores for each rubric item. Patterns, such as consistently low financial-soundness scores, show exactly where to focus your remaining practice time.",
order('Full mock round routine',['Take a timed practice test','Receive a surprise practice problem','Complete 15-minute prep','Give a 10-minute presentation','Score with the rubric','Debrief and set goals'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Strengthening the presentation',{a:{text:'Practice judges say your solution was creative but they could not tell how it would be paid for. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you add?',choices:[['A clear budget section with costs, funding and value','b','Correct. Financially sound solutions earn 10 points.',true],['More creative ideas','b','Address the missing rubric item.',false]]},b:{text:'They also note that you never mentioned prior experience. How do you fix it?',choices:[['Have a member briefly connect the solution to a HOSA project or class knowledge','c','Correct.',true],['Skip it','c','Prior knowledge is worth 15 points.',false]]},c:{text:'Your next presentation addresses every rubric item and finishes at 9:40.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Structure 10 minutes: opening, problem and causes, innovative solution, implementation, budget and value, measures and prior knowledge, close; finish around 9:30–9:45; map every section to a rubric item.','Show understanding (causes, stakeholders, provided data), state your innovation explicitly, explain logically with signposting and connect to specific prior knowledge and experience (15 points each).','Delivery (20 points): voice, stage presence, diction and equal team participation; balance test study (team average counts) with full mock rounds scored by the rubric.'],terms:[['Signposting','Words that tell listeners where the presentation is going.'],['Prior knowledge','Learning and experience applied to a new problem.'],['Mock round','A full practice under competition conditions.']],mcqs:[q('How long is the presentation?','10 minutes','5 minutes','15 minutes','20 minutes','One-minute card.'),q('Which rubric item rewards connecting to past experiences?','Applies previous knowledge and experience','Financially sound','Use of information','Voice','15 points.'),q('How many points is delivery worth?','20','10','15','40','Four items.'),q('Which statement shows innovation explicitly?','“Instead of asking patients to come in, we bring the clinic to them.”','“Our idea is good.”','“We have a plan.”','“This is normal.”','State what is new.'),q('Signposting means:','Using words that show where the presentation is going','Holding up signs','Reading notes','Pointing randomly','First, next, finally.'),q('Why should every member speak?','Team participation is scored and all members must take an active role','It saves time','Judges require five speakers','It is optional','Rule.'),q('Is the team test average part of the final score?','Yes','No','Only for ties','Only at state','Added to presentation.'),q('Where should speakers face?','The judges','The flip charts','The floor','The door','Eye contact.')],frq:{prompt:'Outline a 10-minute presentation for an invented Creative Problem Solving solution, showing how each section earns rubric points.',rubric:[['Structure',['opening','problem','causes','solution','implementation','budget','measures','close','10 minutes']],['Content items',['understanding','innovation','explanation','prior knowledge','financially','provided information']],['Delivery',['voice','eye contact','transitions','every member','fillers']],['Specifics',['data','cost','timeline','experience','example']]],model:'Our invented problem is that missed appointments at a rural clinic rose to 30%. From 0:00 to 1:00, Speaker 1 opens with a hook—“Every missed appointment is a missed chance to catch a problem early”—and names our solution, “Care Connect.” From 1:00 to 2:30, Speaker 2 earns understanding points by stating the problem with the provided data and explaining root causes: 62% of patients cite transportation and many forget appointments booked weeks ahead. From 2:30 to 5:00, Speaker 3 explains the solution and its innovation explicitly: text and postcard reminders plus a weekly telehealth booth at the public library, bringing care to patients instead of requiring travel, and notes why this beats simply adding staff. From 5:00 to 6:30, Speaker 4 explains implementation—a one-month pilot with existing staff, then expansion—pointing to the timeline on our flip chart. From 6:30 to 7:45, Speaker 4 presents the budget: about $4,500 for software, a donated library space, a tablet and a contingency, under the $6,000 limit, compared with roughly $15,000 in revenue lost each year to missed visits. From 7:45 to 9:00, Speaker 5 shares success measures—cutting missed appointments to 15% in six months—and applies prior knowledge: our HOSA chapter raised meeting attendance from 45% to 80% by switching to text reminders. From 9:00 to 9:45, Speaker 1 closes memorably. Every member speaks with eye contact, clear voices and smooth transitions.'},sources:[RULES,OS_MGMT,OS_OB]};
